PASS level_intents: all 66 levels have a specific intent
PASS no_filler_levels: no trivial filler levels after the tutorial
FAIL level_design: level-design review 7/10 (bar 8), 5 level(s) below 6/10: Strong teaching spine. Each unit has one named lesson, the READ rows show the real odds, and the verbs are introduced alone and then combined (Dubki, Kick, Block, Chain, Rush, DRS, fatigue). The reversals (L9, L55, L60) and the boss finales (L22, L55, L66) are the memorable parts. The set loses points in four places. (1) Defence odds are tuned below your own 55-60% target in later chapters, so chapters 3-6 feel punishing. (2) Chain Tackle is numerically no better than a plain hold, and the L47 and L51 ghost-gate units barely separate skilled from casual players. (3) Several 'spikes' and 'teaches' have flat or inverted difficulty. (4) The retention layer (auction, ghost gate, ladder, kit album, seasons) is barely exercised by the content: auctions appear only in L34, L41, L42 and L44, and the Ghost Gate only in L51 and L63. Most units are 'win by 1' variations with no content-level reward or unlock hooks. Curve: Ch1 (L1-L11) is clean: raid, defend, then combine, with a good L9 reversal and an L11 boss with a readable personality. L1 and L2 are near-gifts (99% and 93%), which is fine for onboarding. Ch2 (L12-L22) is the best-paced chapter: Toe, Dubki, Block, empty raids, Do-or-Die, bonus line, revival, a spike and a boss. Ch3 (L23-L33) starts to bite. L29 is the first unfair unit: competent players win only 28/40, and the stop odds are 18-46% against your stated 55-60% target. Ch4 (L34-L44) is the weakest. The Defend Stand teaches (L35, L36, L37, L39) run four in a row with specialist maths that is hard to read, and the auction, the chapter's headline feature, is a throwaway on a dummy mat (L34, L41) and one optional pre-match step in the boss (L44, where it carries no stakes and no pressure). Ch5-Ch6 (L45-L66) pile on: formation themes, the Ghost Gate, fatigue, captains and feints all run at a 60-70% mid-skill win rate, so the player never gets a confidence beat before the next hard unit. Breathers are well placed (L8, L21, L32, L43, L54, L63), but Ghost Duels are absent from Ch1 and the Ghost Gate is taught only once, even though async ghost PvP is the headline hook. Feint and tell is taught twice (L37, L59) and revival twice (L19, L31, L64) with little change. For retention, add per-chapter unlocks tied to the units (a chant, a jersey, an auction card from each boss), and a recurring 'ghost of your own best run' that replays campaign trials. Rebalance chapters 4-6 so teaches sit at 80%+ for mid-skill players and only spikes and bosses fall to 60-70%.
REDESIGN THESE LEVELS (keep the ones not listed):
L29 (5/10): Competent players win only 28/40. Stops are 18-46%, below the 55-60% target, and 'Hold never stops the touch' makes the 2 of 5 depend on way-home luck. → Raise way-home stops ~10 points, cut the kicker's s to 0.45, and make Hold on a Kick also stop 25% of touches.
L46 (5/10): The stated lesson 'strong against every touch' is false: chain 49 vs thigh 52 on Hand, 45 vs ankle 54 on Toe. Chain beats a hold only vs the walk, and risks two men. Players learn to avoid it. Mid-skill 83% but the READ row undermines the teach. → Chain 65+ vs all touches and walks (except Dubki ~10), keep the two-men penalty on a miss, and fix the intent text.
L47 (5/10): Casual 63% and skilled 65% show no skill gradient. Three men and a 2-touch s 0.69 raider means variance decides it. Chain's numbers are weak (40%). → Lower the raider to s 0.5, raise chain ~15 points on walk/dash, and allow 5 raids.
L51 (5/10): Competent 27/40, mid 70%. The plan is known and still the best counter stops only ~55-58%, so 3 of 5 is a coin flip. The gate is the headline PvP feature and this is the only unit on it. It is also CPU-scripted, not a real friend ghost. → Make the matching counter +25, set the goal to stop 3 of 5 with a known plan so it is solvable, and add a gate you set yourself in Ghost Lab.
L65 (5/10): Casual 55%, mid 60%. The Kick 'lands 2 in 3' so an empty raid is built in, and the mat is Hard with 2-touch raids, so most of the loss is variance. → Lift Kick landing to ~80%, drop the CPU to Medium, and set the start at 18-18.
L1 (6/10): 99% win, only one sensible choice; no decision. → Keep as the tutorial but add a 2nd, optional star-raid choice (touch a corner vs the centre) so the first raid asks one real question.
L6 (6/10): Almost the same unit as L4. The 82% vs 100% difference is too small to feel, and the READ gem does not show which defender is better. → Cut the touched man to ~50%, show a per-defender stop % on the cover ring, and give the raider a Hand-only plan on slots 2 and 6 so position matters.
L10 (6/10): Casual 85% and skilled 85% means skill does nothing. It does not feel like a spike. → Raise the CPU to s 0.2 with 2-touch raids, or set 12-14 down. Aim for casual ~70% and skilled ~88%.
L16 (6/10): The 3-star play is 'exactly one empty raid', but best-3★ is 2★ for competent players. Targets are ~35% held on every touch, so the unit is a coin flip. → Drop touch risk by 8-10 points and check that a competent player can reach 3★. Show an empty-raid counter pip on the HUD.
L19 (6/10): Teach unit at 66% mid-skill, while the later spike L31 sits at 73%, so the order is inverted. Stops are 20/50, below the stated 55-60% target. → Raise the stop odds ~10 points, start with 4 on the mat, and keep 'stop 2 of 4'.
L34 (6/10): The headline meta mode is a dummy mat with 6 lots and an obvious answer. Win 73% mid-skill is purse arithmetic, not thought. → Add a rival bidder, an AI that sometimes bids up a lot, and a scouting hint on opponent needs.
L35 (6/10): Competent players win only 31/40 on a teach. The x1.25 bonus is small beside 50% stop variance, so specialists feel marginal. → Raise the specialist bonus to x1.5 and show the stop % per defender on the select ring.
L41 (6/10): The raider is s 0.12 Easy (71% stop), so the defence half is trivial and the auction is a coin flip on an obvious pick. Mid-skill 69% comes from the buy, not the match. → Let the purchased squad matter by making the raider s 0.4, and show the held % per defender after the buy.
L44 (6/10): Competent 29/40, mid 61%. The auction is optional with no clear reward, and a win rate this low makes the auction feel mandatory. → Tune the match to ~75% mid-skill, show a squad-strength meter and give a visible benefit (+stamina/+stop) for each buy.
L50 (6/10): The first close call is rigged 'very tight and wrong', so the lesson is scripted and the review is a free win. Score 4 in 4 raids at ~48% held is tight, and 'fail loses the trial' is harsh for a teach. → Script only the first call, show the confidence band, and allow 5 raids.
L58 (6/10): Competent 29/40, mid 61%. Ankle is 36% on the Dubki, the touch is Hand/Kick, and stopping 3 of 6 at ~30% per stop is near-luck. → Raise Ankle vs Dubki to ~55, or goal stop 2 of 6.
L59 (6/10): A repeat of L37 with a higher tell rate and chain. Mid-skill 67%. → Give the Feint Master a unique rival signature (e.g. the feint comes after a Dash) or cut the unit and fold the chain lesson into L37.
L62 (6/10): The intent says Dubki is the 'one clearly good' way home, but READ shows 44% held after one touch, worse than Walk-after-one at 60%?? no real good option. Dash is 89%. → Cut Dubki to ~25% held, and lower the pressure (start 10-11).
L7 (7/10): Pull is a 46% coin flip with no skill window shown. The unit teaches 'get held and hope'. → Show the perfect-escape window on the ring and make a timed tap lift the pull from 46% to ~65%.
L15 (7/10): Teach at 78%/84% is low, and the dash-vs-walk telegraph odds are not shown, so players guess. → Show the sprinter's dash share (e.g. 70%) on the card and raise the casual win rate to ~85%.
L18 (7/10): Bonus is 4% held: a free point with no tension, so the 'greed' lesson never bites. → Put the bonus at ~15-20% and tie it to the raider's stamina, so one hop is a real choice vs a safe touch.
L21 (7/10): Bonus at 5% is free. Ghost PvP appears first in Ch2, which is late for a headline mode. → Add a Ch1 ghost duel (after L8) against a friendly bot ghost, and raise this bonus to ~15%.
L25 (7/10): Mid-skill 80%. Rush is only good against the hop, so it is a one-trick read. → Add a rusher-biased feint-free second raider or a Rush-vs-walk trap to give 'when not to Rush' more weight.
L27 (7/10): Scouting is a flat +7 on the last-used move, so it reduces to 'never repeat', which is mechanical busywork on a 4-raid unit. Mid-skill 71%. → Show a scouted-tag history line and add a mat where the safest move is the scouted one, so the player has to weigh it.
L28 (7/10): Four targets in four raids with no spare. The goal is nearly identical to L5/L12 'get N points'. → Allow 5 raids, and add a lone-survivor revive tutorial prompt when a caught raid revives their man.

level-design score 7/10 — Strong teaching spine. Each unit has one named lesson, the READ rows show the real odds, and the verbs are introduced alone and then combined (Dubki, Kick, Block, Chain, Rush, DRS, fatigue). The reversals (L9, L55, L60) and the boss finales (L22, L55, L66) are the memorable parts. The set loses points in four places. (1) Defence odds are tuned below your own 55-60% target in later chapters, so chapters 3-6 feel punishing. (2) Chain Tackle is numerically no better than a plain hold, and the L47 and L51 ghost-gate units barely separate skilled from casual players. (3) Several 'spikes' and 'teaches' have flat or inverted difficulty. (4) The retention layer (auction, ghost gate, ladder, kit album, seasons) is barely exercised by the content: auctions appear only in L34, L41, L42 and L44, and the Ghost Gate only in L51 and L63. Most units are 'win by 1' variations with no content-level reward or unlock hooks.
curve: Ch1 (L1-L11) is clean: raid, defend, then combine, with a good L9 reversal and an L11 boss with a readable personality. L1 and L2 are near-gifts (99% and 93%), which is fine for onboarding. Ch2 (L12-L22) is the best-paced chapter: Toe, Dubki, Block, empty raids, Do-or-Die, bonus line, revival, a spike and a boss. Ch3 (L23-L33) starts to bite. L29 is the first unfair unit: competent players win only 28/40, and the stop odds are 18-46% against your stated 55-60% target. Ch4 (L34-L44) is the weakest. The Defend Stand teaches (L35, L36, L37, L39) run four in a row with specialist maths that is hard to read, and the auction, the chapter's headline feature, is a throwaway on a dummy mat (L34, L41) and one optional pre-match step in the boss (L44, where it carries no stakes and no pressure). Ch5-Ch6 (L45-L66) pile on: formation themes, the Ghost Gate, fatigue, captains and feints all run at a 60-70% mid-skill win rate, so the player never gets a confidence beat before the next hard unit. Breathers are well placed (L8, L21, L32, L43, L54, L63), but Ghost Duels are absent from Ch1 and the Ghost Gate is taught only once, even though async ghost PvP is the headline hook. Feint and tell is taught twice (L37, L59) and revival twice (L19, L31, L64) with little change. For retention, add per-chapter unlocks tied to the units (a chant, a jersey, an auction card from each boss), and a recurring 'ghost of your own best run' that replays campaign trials. Rebalance chapters 4-6 so teaches sit at 80%+ for mid-skill players and only spikes and bosses fall to 60-70%.
